{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChK6kxLT8ue4McsNtMmS1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed it: `delete()` now sets the primary key to `None` on instances of models with no dependencies, the same as the normal delete path already does.\n\nThe bug was in the shortcut Django takes when deleting a single object that nothing else depends on (`django/db/models/deletion.py`, around line 280). That branch ran the delete query and returned without clearing the PK. I added one line there that sets the PK attribute to `None` right after the delete query runs.\n\nThe existing `delete` test module passes (41 tests, 1 skipped). I didn't add a new test for this case, since the rules say not to edit test files."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":158,"cache_read_input_tokens":19039,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":158},"output_tokens":194,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3823b6e7-2222-4f03-b9b2-4d4dff2fce57","uuid":"83cdccc7-8f6c-4779-ad8a-5922f0978aaf","timestamp":"2026-11-23T02:50:35.227Z","request_id":"req_011ChK6kwoxWewMhLLVZkzKp"}
